          "informationUri": "https://github.com/manucadev81/arc-porter",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "AP001",
              "name": "prevrandao-randomness",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "block.prevrandao is always 0 on Arc"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "On Arc the PREVRANDAO opcode always returns 0, so block.prevrandao (and its legacy alias block.difficulty) never carries randomness. Lotteries, shuffles and relay selection built on it always get the same value."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use a verifiable random function or a randomness oracle instead of block.prevrandao.\\n\\nSource: https://docs.arc.io/arc/references/evm-differences#execution-and-opcode-differences"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.arc.io/arc/references/evm-differences#execution-and-opcode-differences",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "arc",
                  "portability"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AP002",
              "name": "native-erc20-decimal-mix",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Native value and ERC-20 USDC mixed in one function"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "On Arc msg.value and address.balance are denominated in 18-decimal native USDC, while balanceOf, transfer and transferFrom on the USDC contract use 6 decimals over the same balance. A function that reads or moves both is off by a factor of 10^12 unless it converts."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pick one interface per function. If both are needed, convert explicitly: native = erc20 * 10**12, and never compare or add raw values.\\n\\nSource: https://docs.arc.io/arc/concepts/stablecoin-native-model#usdc-as-the-native-token"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.arc.io/arc/concepts/stablecoin-native-model#usdc-as-the-native-token",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "arc",
                  "portability"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AP003",
              "name": "usdc-18-decimals",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Arc USDC scaled as an 18-decimal token"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The USDC ERC-20 interface at 0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000 uses 6 decimals. A file that hard-codes that address and also scales amounts by 1e18, 10**18 or the ether unit is very likely treating USDC like an 18-decimal token."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Scale USDC amounts by 1e6 (or read decimals() at runtime) when using the ERC-20 interface; reserve 1e18 for native value only.\\n\\nSource: https://docs.arc.io/arc/references/contract-addresses#usdc"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.arc.io/arc/references/contract-addresses#usdc",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "arc",
                  "portability"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AP004",
              "name": "value-to-zero-address",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Value sent to address(0) reverts on Arc"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A value-bearing transfer to the zero address reverts on Arc with \\"Zero address not allowed\\". Because USDC is the native asset and the ERC-20 interface moves the same balance, sending tokens to address(0) as a burn also fails."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Never send value or USDC to address(0). To retire funds, send them to an address you control and account for them there.\\n\\nSource: https://docs.arc.io/arc/references/evm-differences#value-transfer-rules"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.arc.io/arc/references/evm-differences#value-transfer-rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "arc",
                  "portability"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AP005",
              "name": "push-payment-loop",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Transfer inside a loop can be blocked by one recipient"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "On Arc a USDC transfer can revert even when the sender has the balance: the recipient may be blocklisted, the zero address or a self-destructed account. A loop that pushes value to several recipients fails for everyone when one of them reverts."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Credit each recipient in storage and let them withdraw individually, so one address that cannot receive USDC does not block the others.\\n\\nSource: https://docs.arc.io/arc/tutorials/porting-contracts-to-arc#steps"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.arc.io/arc/tutorials/porting-contracts-to-arc#steps",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "arc",
                  "portability"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AP006",
              "name": "selfdestruct-usage",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "selfdestruct moves USDC and reverts in several cases on Arc"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "On Arc a contract's USDC is its native balance, so selfdestruct transfers that USDC to the beneficiary. It reverts when the beneficiary is the contract itself, the zero address, a blocklisted address or an account that already self-destructed, and any later non-zero-value call to the destructed account reverts."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove selfdestruct. Move funds out with an explicit withdrawal and disable the contract with a flag.\\n\\nSource: https://docs.arc.io/arc/references/evm-differences#selfdestruct"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.arc.io/arc/references/evm-differences#selfdestruct",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "arc",
                  "portability"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AP007",
              "name": "wrapped-native-pattern",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "WETH-style wrapper has no place on Arc"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Arc's native asset already exposes an ERC-20 interface, so there is no wrapped native token and no WUSDC. Code that names WETH or WUSDC, or wraps with deposit{value:} and unwraps with withdraw(), carries a step that does not exist on Arc and fragments liquidity if deployed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Replace the WETH constant with the USDC ERC-20 address 0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000 and remove deposit() and withdraw() wrap calls.\\n\\nSource: https://docs.arc.io/arc/tutorials/porting-contracts-to-arc#steps"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.arc.io/arc/tutorials/porting-contracts-to-arc#steps",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
